    <article class="py-12">
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
            <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-700">

                <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-8">A safe is for keeping firearms out of the wrong hands. It is not where your only inventory should live. Two reported thefts this month make that distinction hard to ignore.</p>

                <p>This is not a rewrite of the after-theft steps. If a firearm is already gone, start with <a href="stolen-firearm-checklist.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">What to Do When a Firearm Is Stolen</a>. This post is about the records that have to exist <em>before</em> that day — and that have to survive it.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Clifton Forge, August 12: the safe left the house</h2>
                <p>On August 12, 2026, Alleghany County Sheriff’s Office deputies responded to a reported burglary on Stacks Lane in Clifton Forge, Virginia. Someone had broken into the home and stolen a locked safe containing several firearms.</p>

                <p>The preliminary investigation, as reported by <a href="https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/08/13/burglar-uses-vehicle-remove-safe-full-guns-through-house-wall/" class="text-av-gold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WDBJ</a> and also covered by the <a href="https://virginianreview.com/335268/" class="text-av-gold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Virginian Review</a>, is that a vehicle was used to pull the safe through the exterior wall, causing significant damage. The investigation was ongoing. Anyone with information can contact the Alleghany County Sheriff’s Office at (540) 965-1770 and ask for Investigator Morris.</p>

                <p>The locked safe did not stay in the house. That is the part that matters for records. If the only list of serials, photos, or receipts was inside that safe, there is nothing left to hand police the same day.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Cedartown, August 15: records ready the same day</h2>
                <p>Three days later, on August 15, a resident on Lindsey Chapel Road in Cedartown, Georgia, came home around 3:30 p.m. and found items scattered through the house. There were no signs of forced entry. A keyless-entry gun safe was found open.</p>

                <p>According to <a href="https://coosavalleynews.com/2026/08/cedartown-man-charged-with-theft-3/" class="text-av-gold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coosa Valley News</a>, nine firearms and about 2,000 rounds of ammunition were reported stolen, with an estimated value of $5,000. The resident provided full make, model, and serial number information so the guns could be entered into the Georgia Crime Information Center database. A suspect was later arrested and charged with felony theft by taking and possession of a firearm by a convicted felon.</p>

                <p>That is the other half of the lesson. Records that are ready the same day are what let police enter the guns into the system. A safe that was opened or taken does not do that work by itself.</p>

                <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
                    <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                        <i class="bi bi-lightbulb text-amber-600 text-xl mt-1"></i>
                        <div>
                            <h4 class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-1">A locked safe is not a records plan</h4>
                            <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm mb-0">The Clifton Forge safe left through the wall. The Cedartown safe was found open. In both cases, the hardware failed as a filing cabinet. Keep a copy of your inventory that is not only inside the safe.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Keep a copy that is not only inside the safe</h2>
                <p>Paper in the safe, a notebook on the shelf next to it, or a spreadsheet on a thumb drive stored with the guns all share the same problem: they leave when the safe leaves, or they burn when the house burns.</p>

                <p>You do not need a complicated system. You need one complete copy that still exists if the safe is gone:</p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                    <li>An encrypted digital inventory you can open from another device</li>
                    <li>A printed or exported report stored off-site, with a trusted person, or in a separate location</li>
                    <li>Photos that include serial-number close-ups, not just a group shot of the safe interior</li>
                </ul>

                <p>A locked safe is still the right place for the firearms. It is the wrong place for the only copy of the serials.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">What police need the same day</h2>
                <p>When you call, the useful record is specific. “Several guns from a safe” is a start. Make, model, caliber, and serial number is what turns that start into an entry in a crime database.</p>

                <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-4"><i class="bi bi-check2-square text-av-gold mr-2"></i>Have this ready before you need it</h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Make / manufacturer</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Model</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Caliber or gauge</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> <strong>Serial number</strong></li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Photos, if you have them — overall and serial close-up</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <p>That is the same-day list. Distinguishing marks, optics, and other details help, but they are secondary to being able to identify each firearm. Check your local reporting rules; some jurisdictions set a deadline. This is not legal advice — use local law and the <a href="stolen-firearm-checklist.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">stolen-firearm checklist</a> for the after-theft sequence.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Insurance still wants the report and the proof</h2>
                <p>Police need identifiers. Insurers still want two more things: the police report, and proof of value. Serials get a gun into a database. Receipts, photos, purchase prices, and current estimates are what keep a claim from becoming a negotiation from memory.</p>

                <p>If the only proof of value was also inside the safe, you are reconstructing a claim under stress. That is a harder day than a quiet weekend spent writing the numbers down once.</p>

